---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const link = (p: string) => `${base}${p}`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Pitch"
  description="dot-agents — the operating layer for AI coding agents. A 5-section founder pitch: problem, approach, actions, results, what's next."
>
  <div class="pitch-main">
    <!-- ===================== HERO ===================== -->
    <header class="pitch-hero">
      <div class="eyebrow">dot-agents — founder pitch</div>
      <h1>Stop treating AI coding agents like chat windows. Run them like operators.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        One canonical home for every agent's config. A structured workflow
        loop those agents drive themselves. A knowledge graph so the work
        compounds instead of resetting every session. Built and battle-tested
        on its own codebase.
      </p>

      <nav aria-label="Pitch sections">
        <ol class="pitch-toc">
          <li><a href="#problem"><span class="step">§1 · Problem</span>Why every team rebuilds the same context every morning</a></li>
          <li><a href="#approach"><span class="step">§2 · Approach</span>One canonical home, one operating loop, structured memory</a></li>
          <li><a href="#actions"><span class="step">§3 · Actions</span>The CLI, the workflow surface, the KG, the verifiers</a></li>
          <li><a href="#results"><span class="step">§4 · Results</span>What we can measure, what we can only point at</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next"><span class="step">§5 · Next</span>Background service, scoped KG, layered PR fanout</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <!-- ===================== §1 PROBLEM ===================== -->
    <section id="problem" class="pitch-section">
      <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-num">§1</span>
        <h2>The problem — context resets every session, configs sprawl every machine</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="section-sub">
        Anyone running AI coding agents past hobby scale feels both pains.
        They compound.
      </p>

      <h3>1.1 Config fragmentation</h3>
      <p>
        Every AI coding agent has its own config location and format.
        Cursor wants <code>.cursor/rules/*.mdc</code>. Claude Code wants
        <code>CLAUDE.md</code> and <code>.claude/</code>. Codex wants
        <code>AGENTS.md</code>. GitHub Copilot wants files scattered under
        <code>.github/</code>. OpenCode wants its own. Today, the only way
        to keep a coding standard or skill in sync across all five is
        copy-paste — across N repos and M agents.
      </p>

      <div class="pitch-grid-2">
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">Symptom</span>
          <h3>Rules drift</h3>
          <p>A team-wide convention edits cleanly in one repo's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, then doesn't make it into the other six repos or the two new laptops. By the time someone notices, "the rule" exists in four mutually-contradictory forms.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">Symptom</span>
          <h3>Platform lock-in by accident</h3>
          <p>Once a team writes 30 hard-won rules into Cursor's format, switching to Claude Code or Codex stops being a real option. The friction isn't the agent — it's the config debt.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>1.2 Workflow fragmentation</h3>
      <p>
        Agents already <em>behave</em> like a workflow system — they resume work
        across sessions, persist plans, verify as they go. But every
        platform scatters that state in its own format, in its own place, with
        no contract for the next session to pick it up.
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Pain today</th><th>What it costs</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>30–40 min/session re-explaining yesterday's work</td><td>Direct token + wall-clock loss; staff-engineer attention burned on context reload</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Agents rediscover what's broken every session</td><td>Re-running tests, re-reading errors, re-deriving fixes that were already on disk somewhere</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No safe way to run agents in parallel</td><td>Either serialize (slow) or watch them clobber each other's branches</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tribal knowledge stays tribal</td><td>Why we chose Postgres, why this rule exists, what we tried before — all forgotten</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Agents can't improve their own setup</td><td>Every wave the same mistakes recur until a human encodes the rule</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>1.3 Who feels it</h3>
      <div class="pitch-grid-3">
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">Persona</span>
          <h3>Solo engineer, multi-agent</h3>
          <p>One human, one repo, three agents — Claude in one window, Codex in another, Cursor running locally. Every rule has to be written three times, and nobody owns the canonical version.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">Persona</span>
          <h3>Small team, shared standards</h3>
          <p>Standards-as-prose live in a Notion page nobody reads. Standards-as-config drift across each developer's <code>~/.cursor/</code>. Code review keeps catching the same five anti-patterns.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">Persona</span>
          <h3>Org with parallel agents</h3>
          <p>The next obvious productivity unlock is fanning work out to multiple agents at once. Without bounded write scopes and structured merge-back, parallel agents are a race condition machine.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="pitch-callout">
        <strong>The honest statement of the problem:</strong> agents already
        do the work; the failure mode is the <em>operational layer around them</em> —
        config, state, memory, coordination. Nobody ships that layer. So
        every team builds half of it, badly, repeatedly.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ===================== §2 APPROACH ===================== -->
    <section id="approach" class="pitch-section">
      <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-num">§2</span>
        <h2>The approach — agent-as-operator, with structured boundaries</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="section-sub">
        Three commitments. Each one inverts a default that AI coding tools
        ship with today.
      </p>

      <h3>2.1 One canonical home, projected — not copied</h3>
      <p>
        <code>~/.agents/</code> holds <code>rules/</code>, <code>skills/</code>,
        <code>agents/</code>, <code>hooks/</code>, <code>mcp/</code>, and
        <code>settings/</code>. The <code>da</code> CLI projects them into each
        repo in the shape each platform expects — symlinks where they're
        supported, hard links where they aren't (Cursor), files generated
        from templates where the platform demands its own shape (Copilot).
        Edit once. Every repo, every agent picks it up on the next refresh.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is not "another config tool." It's an explicit rejection of the
        idea that each agent's config format gets its own source of truth.
        Multi-agent is the world; the canonical home is the substrate.
      </p>

      <h3>2.2 The agent runs the operating layer, not the human</h3>
      <p>
        The previous default — humans learn a workflow CLI, then drive
        their agent through it — multiplies cognitive load instead of
        reducing it. dot-agents inverts the loop:
      </p>
      <div class="pitch-grid-2">
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">Before</span>
          <h3>CLI-first</h3>
          <p>Human runs <code>da sync</code>, <code>da refresh</code>, <code>da workflow resume</code>. The agent benefits from managed state, but the human owns every command.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">After</span>
          <h3>Agent-first with escape hatches</h3>
          <p>Agent runs <code>da workflow orient</code> at session start, <code>checkpoint</code> at breakpoints, <code>workflow fanout</code> when it needs help. <code>da review</code> is the one command the human owns — approve/reject what the agent proposes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The <em>approval gradient</em> matters more than the command list:
        agents auto-apply safe operations (checkpoint state, persist verification
        results, write lessons). They <em>propose</em> things that change shared
        state (new rules, skill edits, workflow config). They <em>escalate</em>
        the rest. Humans steer; agents handle the mechanical work.
      </p>

      <h3>2.3 Bounded delegation with structured merge-back</h3>
      <p>
        Parallel agents are the next obvious productivity unlock — and the
        next obvious way to corrupt your tree. dot-agents treats every fan-out
        as a contract: an explicit <em>write scope</em> (which files this
        agent may touch), a verification clause, a structured merge-back
        artifact, and a parent who closes out the task once the work lands.
        Worktrees isolate filesystems; bundles isolate intent; the
        orchestrator is the only thing that crosses those boundaries.
      </p>

      <h3>2.4 Memory that compounds — the knowledge graph</h3>
      <p>
        A local KG (sqlite by default, Postgres-backed for shared scopes)
        holds three things: structured notes (decisions, entities, lessons),
        a code graph keyed off Tree-sitter, and a bridge between the two so
        a decision can pin itself to a function or a file. The
        <em>scoped</em> KG (§5) adds <code>repo / user / team / org / public</code>
        as a precedence chain — a team convention can outrank an
        org-wide default without leaking into someone else's repo graph.
      </p>

      <div class="pitch-callout">
        The philosophy: <strong>treat agents as operators with shape</strong> —
        bounded scopes, structured state, durable memory, explicit human
        review gates. Not chat windows you nudge.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ===================== §3 ACTIONS ===================== -->
    <section id="actions" class="pitch-section">
      <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-num">§3</span>
        <h2>The actions — what's shipped, today, on master</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="section-sub">
        Concrete moves. Each one is in the binary or the repo; each one
        is reachable by a command you can run right now.
      </p>

      <h3>3.1 The <code>da</code> CLI</h3>
      <p>
        Single Go binary. Cobra subcommands. Reaches every part of the
        operating layer.
      </p>
      <div class="pitch-grid-2">
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">Config surface</span>
          <h3>init · add · refresh · install · doctor · status · explain</h3>
          <p>Bootstrap the canonical home, adopt a repo, re-project after pulls, sanity-check links and projections, surface a human-readable explanation of what's managed where.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">Workflow surface</span>
          <h3>{'workflow { orient · next · eligible · fanout · merge-back · delegation closeout · fold-back · checkpoint · verify · advance · plan · plan archive }'}</h3>
          <p>The full plan / task / slice / verification / delegation / fold-back surface — explained in the live workflow walkthrough.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">Resource surface</span>
          <h3>rules · skills · agents · hooks · mcp · settings</h3>
          <p>Each managed resource has the same six subcommands (list / add / remove / show / promote / sync) per the resource-command contract.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">Review surface</span>
          <h3>da review</h3>
          <p>The one command humans really need. Inspect a pending proposal; approve it (it lands in <code>~/.agents/</code>); reject it (it gets archived with the reason). Scoped to global by default; project-local proposal support is on the roadmap.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>3.2 The skills layer — durable agent procedures</h3>
      <p>
        Skills sit between rules ("always-on guidelines") and agents
        ("subagent definitions"). A skill is an on-demand procedure: at a
        certain trigger, load this prompt and these tools. Canonical
        examples shipped today:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>orchestrator-session-start</strong> — eligible-orient → KG readback → fanout-vs-direct decision → bundle authoring.</li>
        <li><strong>loop-worker</strong> — bounded implementation worker for a delegated task. Cold-start capable; reads a bundle, implements the write scope, calls <code>iteration-close</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>iteration-close</strong> — verify → checkpoint → merge-back / advance. Fixes the canonical <em>persisted_via_workflow_commands: no</em> anti-pattern.</li>
        <li><strong>delegation-lifecycle</strong> — the contract a delegating agent commits to: bundle → spawn → merge-back → closeout.</li>
        <li><strong>plan-wave-picker</strong> · <strong>isp</strong> · <strong>review-pr</strong> · <strong>build-graph</strong> · <strong>review-delta</strong> — the rest of the working surface.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>3.3 Hooks — the lifecycle automation</h3>
      <p>
        Hooks fire at platform-defined moments (session start, pre-commit,
        post-test, on-error). They're how dot-agents stays out of the
        human's way: the orient hook loads context at session start; the
        post-commit hook updates the code graph; the stop hook runs the
        verifier and gates the next iteration.
      </p>

      <h3>3.4 The knowledge graph — <code>da kg</code></h3>
      <p>Two integrated layers:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Notes</strong> — structured frontmatter + body, typed
        (<code>decision</code>, <code>entity</code>, <code>lesson</code>),
        addressable by intent (<code>decision_lookup</code>,
        <code>entity_recall</code>). Warm-store row in sqlite/Postgres;
        hot-file source-of-truth on disk.</li>
        <li><strong>Code graph</strong> — Tree-sitter symbol/import/call
        graph bridged into the same store. Lets the orchestrator
        pre-flight a write scope against the code graph before fanout
        (lesson: <a href={link('/canonical/lessons/bundle-scope-via-code-graph/')}><code>bundle-scope-via-code-graph</code></a>) and lets reviewers see
        blast-radius before approving a change.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The bridge is contract-stable: see the
        <a href={link('/canonical/specs/graph-bridge-contract/')}><code>graph-bridge-contract</code></a>
        spec. An MCP server exposes the whole surface to every platform that
        speaks MCP.
      </p>

      <h3>3.5 The verifier — shift-left CI ownership</h3>
      <p>
        Earlier waves asked the impl agent to babysit the PR — write the
        code <em>and</em> watch SonarCloud, fix mechanical issues, re-run
        until green. That model burned 5–10× the necessary context per PR.
        Today a <code>pr-ci</code> verifier profile (configured in
        <code>.agentsrc.json</code>) owns the CI watch loop. The impl agent
        exits at merge-back; the verifier auto-fixes cog, dup, cov, and
        allowlist issues; only the genuinely-novel failures escalate as a
        structured fix-up brief.
      </p>

      <h3>3.6 The fold-back and proposal loop — the system improves itself</h3>
      <p>
        When a worker notices its bundle would push it outside its write
        scope, it doesn't silently expand — it emits a
        <em>fold-back</em>. The fold-back becomes a task in the plan; the
        orchestrator schedules it next wave. When the agent notices a
        recurring pattern worth codifying, it queues a
        <em>proposal</em> — a structured artifact under
        <code>~/.agents/proposals/</code> (global scope) or
        <code>.agents/proposals/</code> (project-local). The human runs
        <code>da review</code>; approved proposals flow into the canonical
        home and the next session's orient picks them up.
      </p>

      <h3>3.7 The lessons corpus — durable mistake memory</h3>
      <p>
        <code>.agents/lessons/&lt;name&gt;/LESSON.md</code> entries
        capture mistakes that wasted real time. Every lesson has a
        consistent shape — pattern, root cause, rule, cross-references.
        The corpus is the working memory of what <em>not</em> to do.
        We're at <strong>34 lessons and counting</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="pitch-deeplinks">
        <h3>Live walkthroughs (existing demo material)</h3>
        <div class="cards">
          <a class="card" href={link('/demos/demo_readme/')}>
            <span class="label">One-minute overview</span>
            <span class="title">DEMO_README</span>
          </a>
          <a class="card" href={link('/demos/demo_diagram/')}>
            <span class="label">Architecture + loop</span>
            <span class="title">DEMO_DIAGRAM</span>
          </a>
          <a class="card" href={link('/demos/demo_workflow_walkthrough/')}>
            <span class="label">End-to-end wave (PRs #118/#130/#133/#135)</span>
            <span class="title">DEMO_WORKFLOW_WALKTHROUGH</span>
          </a>
          <a class="card" href={link('/demos/demo_lessons_narrative/')}>
            <span class="label">7 hard-won lessons, story-format</span>
            <span class="title">DEMO_LESSONS_NARRATIVE</span>
          </a>
          <a class="card" href={link('/demos/demo_index/')}>
            <span class="label">All demo material</span>
            <span class="title">DEMO_INDEX</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ===================== §4 RESULTS ===================== -->
    <section id="results" class="pitch-section">
      <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-num">§4</span>
        <h2>The results — what changed, with evidence</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="section-sub">
        Honest about what's measured vs. claimed. Everything below is
        either a count we can point at on this repo, or a pattern we can
        trace through merged PRs and lesson files.
      </p>

      <h3>4.1 Repeat-mistake reduction (counted)</h3>
      <p>
        The lessons corpus is the canonical evidence channel. Each entry
        names a mistake that wasted time, the root cause, and the rule
        that prevents it. After a lesson lands as a rule, hook, or skill,
        the same class of mistake stops recurring. Examples:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The <code>cd</code> that swapped two branches</strong>
          (<a href={link('/canonical/lessons/worktree-no-cd/')}><code>worktree-no-cd</code></a>) — was a cross-worktree
          contamination bug. After the lesson became a rule, no
          subsequent multi-worktree session has reproduced it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Impl agents re-learning the Sonar API every PR</strong>
          (<a href={link('/canonical/lessons/verifier-owns-ci-watch-shift-left/')}><code>verifier-owns-ci-watch-shift-left</code></a>) — the <code>pr-ci</code>
          verifier profile encodes that knowledge once. Per the lesson:
          <em>next wave runs ~3–5× cheaper</em> (token estimate, see §4.4 caveat).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Stale TASKS.yaml driving fanout</strong>
          (<a href={link('/canonical/lessons/verify-task-status-vs-pr-history/')}><code>verify-task-status-vs-pr-history</code></a>) — was a near-miss
          ship-a-revert incident. The pre-fanout reconciliation rule has
          held since.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>4.2 Throughput (counted)</h3>
      <div class="pitch-grid-3">
        <div class="pitch-stat">
          <span class="num">143</span>
          <span class="lbl">Merged PRs on dot-agents itself</span>
          <span class="src">Source: <code>gh pr list --state merged --limit 200</code>, as of this build</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-stat">
          <span class="num">61</span>
          <span class="lbl">Plans archived to <code>.agents/history/</code></span>
          <span class="src">Source: <code>ls .agents/history/</code></span>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-stat">
          <span class="num">34</span>
          <span class="lbl">Lessons captured + cross-referenced</span>
          <span class="src">Source: <code>.agents/lessons/index.md</code></span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        dot-agents is the primary case study for itself. Every PR went
        through the orient → act → persist → delegate → propose loop the
        product ships. Every lesson is a real mistake that became a real
        rule. Every archived plan is a real wave of work — bundles, merge-backs,
        verifications, the whole shape.
      </p>

      <h3>4.3 Pattern → platform behavior (traced)</h3>
      <p>
        The most important result isn't a number — it's that the system
        improves itself in a way you can trace. One worked example:
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Artifact</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Pattern noticed in a wave</td><td>Impl agents kept re-learning SonarCloud's API and the <code>gh pr checks</code> schema, eating context.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lesson captured</td><td><a href={link('/canonical/lessons/verifier-owns-ci-watch-shift-left/')}><code>verifier-owns-ci-watch-shift-left</code></a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Proposal queued</td><td><a href={link('/canonical/proposals/pr-ci-verifier-integration-audit/')}><code>pr-ci-verifier-integration-audit</code></a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Reviewed + approved</td><td><code>da review approve</code> → landed in <code>~/.agents/</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Behavior now baked in</td><td><code>pr-ci</code> verifier profile in <code>.agentsrc.json</code>; impl agents exit at merge-back; next wave consistently cheaper.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        This shape — observation → lesson → proposal → review → canonical
        change — has repeated through at least the seven lessons featured in
        the lessons narrative, and is the operating-loop's most-load-bearing
        claim.
      </p>

      <h3>4.4 What we'd measure but can't yet (the honest gap)</h3>
      <div class="pitch-honest">
        <strong>Caveat:</strong> the "~3–5× cheaper" and "30–40 min/session"
        numbers above are <em>operator estimates from a single maintainer's
        session log</em>, not instrumented telemetry. We don't yet measure
        tokens-per-PR, wall-clock-per-task, or recurrence-rate-by-lesson
        with anything more rigorous than memory. That instrumentation is
        exactly what the <strong><a href={link('/canonical/specs/agent-run-scoring-observability-platform/')}>agent-run-scoring observability
        platform</a></strong> (R2–R5 in §5) is being built to provide.
      </div>

      <h3>4.5 Multi-platform parity (shipped)</h3>
      <p>
        Five platforms ship today: <strong>Cursor</strong>,
        <strong>Claude Code</strong>, <strong>Codex CLI</strong>,
        <strong>GitHub Copilot</strong>, <strong>OpenCode</strong>. The
        <code>platform-driven-diagnostics</code> wave (PRs #118/#130/#133/#135)
        was the canonical migration: per-platform diagnostics moved off a
        central lifecycle table onto platform-owned sister interfaces
        (<code>BrokenLinkReporter</code>, <code>StatusBadger</code>,
        <code>LinkCounter</code>). Adding a sixth platform is now a known
        shape — implement three interfaces, no edits to <code>lifecycle/</code>.
        See the <a href={link('/demos/demo_workflow_walkthrough/')}>walkthrough</a>
        for the wave-by-wave trace.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ===================== §5 NEXT STEPS ===================== -->
    <section id="next" class="pitch-section">
      <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-num">§5</span>
        <h2>What's next — near, mid, far</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="section-sub">
        Tied to in-flight specs in the repo. Every entry below either has
        an accepted spec under <code>.agents/workflow/specs/</code> or a
        plan-design under <code>.agents/workflow/plans/</code>.
      </p>

      <div class="pitch-timeline">
        <div class="lane">
          <span class="horizon">Near · in-flight</span>
          <h3>Layered PR fanout</h3>
          <p>
            Downstream tasks become eligible the moment an upstream PR is
            open and green — not when it's merged. Decouples implementation
            velocity from maintainer review cadence; encodes the wave
            pattern the orchestrator already runs mentally.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Status name: <code>awaiting_review</code> with <code>agent</code>/<code>owner</code> sub-states.</li>
            <li>Verifier/lens split — objective signals unblock eligibility, subjective signals gate merge.</li>
            <li>Self-correcting topology — merges fast-forward downstream; rejections cascade-block visibly.</li>
            <li>Spec: <a href={link('/canonical/specs/layered-pr-fanout/')}><code>layered-pr-fanout</code></a> (accepted 2026-05-28).</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="lane">
          <span class="horizon">Mid · spec accepted</span>
          <h3>Scoped knowledge graphs</h3>
          <p>
            The KG today is single-scope. The next pass models the
            developer's actual world: <code>repo → user → team → org → public</code>
            as an ordered precedence chain. Reads fan out; writes target
            exactly one scope. Every result carries its origin.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Event-driven staleness (not clock-based) — a fact stays true until something invalidates it.</li>
            <li>Per-scope backends — sqlite locally, Postgres for shared, HTTP for read-only public.</li>
            <li>Cross-scope contradictions surface as read-time metadata, not stale tags.</li>
            <li>Spec: <a href={link('/canonical/specs/scoped-knowledge-graphs/')}><code>scoped-knowledge-graphs</code></a>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="lane">
          <span class="horizon">Mid · plan-design ready</span>
          <h3>R3 background-worker service</h3>
          <p>
            <code>da service run</code> — a long-running process that
            hosts the telemetry-ingestion watcher, the scoring-recompute
            task, and the HTTP surface for the dashboard (R2) and
            review/labeling collection (R5). In-process scheduler;
            fsnotify + cron triggers; YAML watermark sidecars for
            durability. Foreground; daemonization is the operator's call.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Single binary, single subcommand. Existing <code>da</code> CLI machinery.</li>
            <li>Pub/sub bus so the dashboard updates in real time, not by polling.</li>
            {/* TODO: needs canonical route — plans are not yet a canonical bucket (only docs/lessons/specs/proposals). */}
            <li>Plan: <code>.agents/workflow/plans/r3-background-worker-service/design.md</code>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>5.2 What's further out</h3>
      <div class="pitch-grid-2">
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">Far · research-stage</span>
          <h3>Dream-cycle / background consolidation</h3>
          <p>
            Idle-time agent work: replay yesterday's sessions, look for
            patterns the active loop missed, propose new lessons, prune
            stale notes. Modeled on memory consolidation in the
            articles-evaluation research. Research lives in
            <a href={link('/canonical/proposals/research-auto-dream-and-background-tasks/')}><code>research-auto-dream-and-background-tasks</code></a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">Far · design pass</span>
          <h3>Two-tier config distribution</h3>
          <p>
            <code>sources/</code> + <code>extends/</code> + <code>packages/</code>
            model so an org can publish a base config, a team can layer
            its own, and a repo can locally override — auditable, version-pinned,
            with explicit conflict semantics.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">Far · design pass</span>
          <h3>Agent-run scoring &amp; observability (R2 / R5)</h3>
          <p>
            Dashboard + review/labeling for every iteration the loop runs.
            Closes the §4.4 gap: replaces operator memory with instrumented
            telemetry. R3 (above) is the hosting substrate. Spec:
            <a href={link('/canonical/specs/agent-run-scoring-observability-platform/')}><code>agent-run-scoring-observability-platform</code></a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-card">
          <span class="small">Far · design pass</span>
          <h3>Project-local <code>da review</code></h3>
          <p>
            Today <code>da review</code> processes global proposals
            (<code>~/.agents/proposals/</code>) only. Project-local
            proposals live alongside as markdown drafts. Roadmap: the same
            schema and CLI surface, scoped to a repo's <code>.agents/</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>5.3 What's intentionally <em>not</em> on the roadmap</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>An "all-in-one" platform UI.</strong> The agent is the UI. The dashboard is for humans to <em>review</em>, not <em>operate</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>A new agent runtime.</strong> dot-agents projects into Cursor, Claude Code, Codex, Copilot, OpenCode — the agents are the runtimes; we're the operating layer between them.</li>
        <li><strong>A hosted SaaS by default.</strong> Local-first is a commitment: sqlite, files on disk, your repo. Hosted scopes (team/org KG, observability) are opt-in.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="pitch-callout">
        <strong>Closing frame.</strong> dot-agents is the substrate.
        Your rules, skills, plans, and the institutional memory around
        them are the product. The CLI gets out of your way; the
        canonical home stays honest; the loop keeps working while you
        sleep.
      </div>

